# November 22, 2026: a third scatter of places across Colorado (region co), claimed by tools/choose.py --thinnest because
# Colorado had the fewest places on the map (34). Sixteen places spread over the state, none of them already on the map:
# Amache on the eastern plains, the Limon depot, the Cokedale coal camp near Trinidad, the Como roundhouse in South Park,
# the Healy House in Leadville, the Marble mill site, the Ute-Ulay mine and mill at Lake City, the Lewis Mill above
# Telluride, the Beaumont Hotel in Ouray, the Strater Hotel in Durango, Our Lady of Guadalupe at Conejos, Hope Lutheran
# Church in Westcliffe, the Museum of Colorado Prisons in Canon City, the Victor downtown district, the Hotel Colorado in
# Glenwood Springs and the Stanley Hotel in Estes Park. Sources were opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text and the
# Register lists, and the pages of the National Park Service, History Colorado, the South Park Rail Society, HMdb,
# Colorado Preservation, the Town of Limon, Visit Telluride, SAH Archipedia, the Denver Gazette, the Sopris Sun, the Pikes
# Peak Library District and the hotels' own sites.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'co_pmqu','label':'Colorado: a third scatter','added':'2026-11-22 18:24'}
PLACES=[
N('amache-granada-co','Amache (Granada War Relocation Center)','Granada','co','inst','ruins',38.04962,-102.3286,'area','public','walk','','1942–1945',
  'One of the ten camps where the War Relocation Authority held Japanese Americans removed from the West Coast after Pearl Harbor. It opened on August 27, 1942, and by February 1943 held 7,318 people, the smallest of the ten camps; more than 10,000 passed through it in its three years. Those held there farmed enough to feed the camp. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 2006 and joined the National Park System as Amache National Historic Site in 2024.',
  'The National Park Service lists a cemetery, a monument, concrete building foundations, the camp\'s intact road grid, and restored and rebuilt camp buildings, among them a barrack and a recreation hall, with panels round the site. In the cemetery in the southwest corner a stone memorial carries the names of 31 men from Amache who died in the war. The barrack and recreation hall can be entered only when park staff are there.',
  'The road grid and foundations of a wartime camp for Japanese Americans on the high plains.',
  'Amache National Historic Site, National Park Service. The NPS page (read November 2026): open in daylight and not gated; no trails, uneven ground with cactus, yucca and rattlesnakes; no restrooms or water; no visitor centre (the Amache Museum in Granada, run by the Amache Preservation Society, has exhibits and the passport stamp). From Granada go west on U.S. 50/385 for a mile and turn left at the Amache sign onto County Road 23 5/10. OpenStreetMap maps a lot on that road.',
  [W('Granada War Relocation Center'),L('NPS: Amache basic information','https://www.nps.gov/amch/planyourvisit/basicinfo.htm'),L('NPS: Amache directions','https://www.nps.gov/amch/planyourvisit/directions.htm')],
  th=[38.05611,-102.32912],thn='Lot on County Road 23 5/10 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',hours='Daylight hours; buildings open only when park staff are present'),
N('limon-depot-co','Limon depot (Limon Heritage Museum)','Limon','co','rail','standing',39.26023,-103.68776,'site','open','drive','stations','1910',
  'Limon grew where the Rock Island Line, building west in 1888, crossed the Kansas Pacific (later Union Pacific); from 1889 the Rock Island ran on to Denver over Union Pacific track, and the town became a junction where trains such as the Rocky Mountain Rocket split for Denver and Colorado Springs. The 1910 depot served both railroads. It was listed on the National Register in 2003 and is one of seven Rock Island stations left standing in Colorado, the only one restored.',
  'The restored depot holds the Town of Limon\'s heritage museum: a railroad exhibit, a restored railroad office of about 1970, a working N-scale model of the 1940s yard and a Native American collection. The Railroad Park beside it has a one-room schoolhouse, a dining car, a Union Pacific caboose, windmills and old farm machinery.',
  'A restored union depot where two railroads met on the plains.',
  'Run by the Town of Limon and the heritage society; 899 1st Street. The town\'s page (read November 2026) gives the season and hours and says admission is free; it was closed for the season when read, with off-season tours through the Limon Chamber of Commerce.',
  [W('Limon station'),L('Town of Limon: Heritage Museum Complex','https://townoflimon.com/index.php/local-info/culture/heritage-museum')],brick='no',
  hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day, Thursday to Sunday 1 to 7 p.m.'),
N('cokedale-coal-camp','Cokedale coal camp and coke ovens','Cokedale','co','town','standing',37.14364,-104.62088,'site','view','drive','','1906–1947',
  'The American Smelting and Refining Company built Cokedale from 1906 as a model company coal camp, to a plan by the Denver architect James Murdoch: rows of workers\' houses along Reilly Creek, with a store, boarding house, school and mine office, most of them of cinder blocks made from coke and cement on sandstone foundations. Coal from Reilly Canyon was coked in ovens across the highway. Most camps like it were pulled down after the First World War, but Cokedale ran as a company town until the mine shut in 1947 and was incorporated in 1948. The district was listed on the National Register in 1985.',
  'SAH Archipedia: over 100 of the original buildings survive, many enlarged since they were sold; the school, mercantile, boarding house and mine office still anchor the plan, and the old Gotlieb mercantile is a museum. A long row of stone coke oven ruins stands in a field across State Highway 12 (Denver Gazette, 2022), about 500 m southeast of the pin beside the highway. The houses are lived in. Museum hours not checked.',
  'The most intact coal company town in Colorado, with its coke ovens across the road.',
  'A small incorporated town on State Highway 12, about 7 to 8 miles west of Trinidad; the houses are private homes, so look from the streets. Who owns the ground under the coke ovens was not checked; the federal land map puts a mapped lot by the highway about 1 km southwest on State Land Board land. View the ovens from the highway.',
  [W('Cokedale Historic District'),W('Cokedale, Colorado'),L('SAH Archipedia: Cokedale Historic District','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/9029'),L('Denver Gazette: Coke ovens among living history at this model camp','https://www.denvergazette.com/2022/05/15/coke-ovens-among-living-history-at-this-model-camp-colorful-colorado/')],brick='no'),
N('como-roundhouse','Como roundhouse, depot and hotel','Como','co','rail','standing',39.31587,-105.89115,'site','view','drive','stations','1879–1939',
  'The Denver, South Park and Pacific reached Como in 1879, and the town became a division point for trains over Boreas Pass and Trout Creek Pass. The stone roundhouse was built by Italian masons in 1881 with six stalls; wooden additions brought it to 19 stalls by 1900. After the railroad left in 1939 the stone part was used for other things, a sawmill in the 1950s among them. The roundhouse, depot and hotel were listed on the National Register together in 1983.',
  'The six-stall stone roundhouse, the depot (saved from collapse with History Colorado\'s help) and the railway hotel, which takes guests again. The South Park Rail Society has leased the roundhouse since 2016 and is rebuilding part of the yard round it and making the building a museum; on open-house days the roundhouse and its railroad equipment can be toured.',
  'An 1881 stone roundhouse in a near-ghost railroad town of South Park.',
  'Roundhouse leased to the South Park Rail Society. Its events page (read November 2026) lists open houses and work days in summer (in 2026 from June to late September, with Boreas Pass Railroad Day on August 15, doors at 9 a.m., free parking); at other times look from the street. OpenStreetMap maps a lot on Old Colorado Highway 8 about 140 m northwest.',
  [L('South Park Rail Society: the roundhouse','https://southparkrailsociety.org/the-roundhouse'),L('South Park Rail Society: events','https://www.southparkrailsociety.org/events'),W('Como, Colorado')],
  th=[39.31775,-105.89264],thn='Lot on Old Colorado Highway 8 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('healy-house-leadville','Healy House and Dexter Cabin','Leadville','co','landmark','standing',39.2518475,-106.2926843,'exact','open','drive','','1878',
  'The mining engineer August R. Meyer built this Greek Revival clapboard house in 1878 for his bride Emma. Daniel Healy bought it in 1888 and ran it as a boarding house with his cousin Nellie Healy, who kept it until 1936 and then gave it to the historical association. Beside it stands the log cabin the mining investor James V. Dexter built in 1879, plain outside and plush within, where he kept a famously exclusive poker club. History Colorado runs both as a museum.',
  'The restored house with Victorian furnishings gathered from Leadville pioneers, the Dexter cabin, and gardens with urns, statuary and a gazebo that visitors may walk in. House tours last an hour; the upper floor is reached only by stairs.',
  'A boom-town boarding house and a millionaire\'s log poker cabin.',
  'History Colorado, 912 Harrison Avenue. Its page (read November 2026) gives the hours below and says parking is free on site; tours after November 30 are booked by email.',
  [W('Healy House Museum'),L('History Colorado: Healy House Museum and Dexter Cabin','https://www.historycolorado.org/node/3671')],brick='no',
  hours='Friday to Sunday 10:30 a.m. to 2:30 p.m.; house tours 11:30 a.m. and 1 p.m. through November 29'),
N('marble-mill-site','Marble mill site','Marble','co','industry','ruins',39.069167,-107.191944,'site','public','walk','','1907',
  'The Colorado Yule Marble Company built its finishing mill on the Crystal River between 1907 and 1910, a building 1,300 feet long that ran for almost half a century. Yule marble from the quarry above town went into the outside of the Lincoln Memorial and the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier. Marble had about 1,500 people at its peak between 1912 and 1915. The 28-acre mill site was listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'Crane pillars of Yule marble blocks, 10 feet square at the base and 20 feet high, and two free-standing marble walls 40 feet long and 30 feet high (Sopris Sun, 2026). The town worried in 2024 that the structures might be unsafe and has had them assessed: keep back from the walls.',
  'Marble walls and crane pillars of the mill that cut the Lincoln Memorial stone.',
  'A park of the Town of Marble on the Crystal River. Hours and rules not checked: the Marble Historical Society page could not be opened when this was written. OpenStreetMap maps a lot on West 3rd Street about 100 m northeast.',
  [W('Marble, Colorado'),L('Sopris Sun: Marble\'s Mill Site makeover','https://soprissun.com/marbles-mill-site-makeover/')],
  th=[39.06967,-107.19089],thn='Lot on West 3rd Street (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('ute-ulay-mine-mill','Ute-Ulay mine and mill and Henson town site','Lake City','co','industry','standing',38.01967,-107.37698,'site','view','drive','','1871–1984',
  'Four prospectors found the parallel silver and lead veins in August 1871 while trespassing on Ute land, and named them Ute and Ulay (for Ouray, the Ute leader); they staked them again in 1874 after the Brunot Agreement. Crooke Mining and Smelting paid a record $125,000 for them in 1876, and between 1874 and 1903 they produced more than $12 million in metals, the backbone of Lake City. Work went on now and then until 1984; a power plant, machine shop and dam were added between 1925 and 1930. LKA Gold gave the town site and then the mill to Hinsdale County in 2012 and 2013.',
  'Colorado Preservation lists 18 structures: cabins, a blacksmith shop, the boarding house, a red-cedar water tank and the assay office, with the headframe, powerhouse and mill buildings on the county\'s 12 acres. Many are unstable, and when that page was written the public was not allowed near the buildings. The HMdb marker says the county and its partners are making it a heritage site.',
  'One of the most complete silver mining camps left in the San Juans.',
  'Owned by Hinsdale County, on County Road 20 about four miles west of Lake City on the Alpine Loop (Denver7). Whether visitors may now walk among the buildings was not checked: look from the road and keep to any posted paths.',
  [L('HMdb: Ute-Ulay','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=177908'),L('Colorado Preservation: Ute Ulay Mill and Town Site','https://www.coloradopreservation.org/?p=8184'),L('Denver7: exploring the Ute-Ulay mining complex','https://www.denver7.com/lifestyle/discover-colorado/colorado-ghost-towns-and-mining-history-exploring-the-ute-ulay-mining-complex-and-town-site')],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('lewis-mill-telluride','Lewis Mill','Telluride','co','industry','standing',37.87889,-107.775,'site','public','hike','','1910',
  'A 60-ton ore concentration mill built in 1910 at the head of Bridal Veil Basin, 3.5 miles southeast of Telluride and at 12,450 feet. It was listed on the National Register in 2009.',
  'Visit Telluride describes it as a preserved five-story structure, reached past other mining remains, waterfalls and wildflowers; Lewis Lake is about a mile beyond. Interior not checked.',
  'A five-story ore mill at 12,450 feet above Bridal Veil Falls.',
  'On Uncompahgre National Forest land (federal land map). Visit Telluride gives the route: up the switchbacked road to the top of Bridal Veil Falls at the east end of Telluride (high-clearance four-wheel drive recommended, or walk it), through the gate into Bridal Veil Basin, right at the fork after about 1.8 miles, left at the Wasatch junction after another 1.5 miles; about 8 miles out and back, 5 to 6 hours, difficult. The parking point is a lot OpenStreetMap maps on the falls road, on National Forest land.',
  [W('Lewis Mill (Telluride, Colorado)'),L('Visit Telluride: Lewis Mine hike','https://telluride.com/activity/lewis-mine')],
  th=[37.91937,-107.76785],thn='Lot on the Bridal Veil road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('beaumont-hotel-ouray','Beaumont Hotel','Ouray','co','hotel','standing',38.02194,-107.67125,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1886',
  'Built in 1886 and opened on July 25, 1887, with staff from Chicago\'s Palmer House and furniture by Marshall Field\'s; it was among the first hotels in the country wired for alternating current, and Theodore Roosevelt and Herbert Hoover stayed. It closed in 1964 and stood empty and boarded for over 30 years, part of the roof falling in. Sold in 1998 and restored with just 12 guest rooms, it reopened in 2003.',
  'A three-story brick hotel mixing Victorian, Queen Anne and Second Empire, with a slate mansard roof; inside, a grand staircase rises to the lobby and a rosewood dining room fills almost half the second floor (Wikipedia). It is a working hotel and spa.',
  'An 1886 mining-town grand hotel that stood boarded up for decades.',
  'A working hotel and spa in downtown Ouray (its own site, read November 2026). Visiting hours for people not staying were not checked.',
  [W('Beaumont Hotel (Ouray, Colorado)'),L('Beaumont Hotel and Spa','https://www.beaumonthotel.com/')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('strater-hotel-durango','Strater Hotel','Durango','co','hotel','standing',37.27112,-107.8818,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1887',
  'The pharmacist Henry Strater and his family built this hotel for $70,000; it opened in 1887, with Strater\'s pharmacy in the corner. It went bankrupt after the Panic of 1893 and was taken over by the Bank of Cleveland. The Barker family renovated it from 1981. It is a contributing building in Durango\'s Main Avenue Historic District.',
  'A working hotel of 88 rooms filled with American Victorian walnut antiques, a high Victorian bar, and the Diamond Belle saloon with costumed staff and an upright grand piano. Louis L\'Amour asked for room 222, above the saloon (Wikipedia).',
  'An 1887 hotel still full of Victorian walnut, with a ragtime saloon.',
  'A working hotel on Main Avenue in Durango (Wikipedia). Its own history page could not be opened when this was written; hours of the saloon not checked.',
  [W('Strater Hotel')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('our-lady-of-guadalupe-conejos','Our Lady of Guadalupe Church','Conejos','co','worship','standing',37.088271,-106.020181,'exact','view','drive','','1858; present church 1927 and 1948',
  'Settlers from Taos founded the parish in 1858, the first Roman Catholic parish in what is now Colorado. An adobe church of 1863 here partly burned in 1926; the back of the present building dates from 1927 and was joined to the surviving 1879 adobe towers and front. In 1948 those towers and the front were taken down, the nave lengthened one bay, and the present front and towers built. It was listed on the National Register in 2018.',
  'History Colorado: a Spanish Colonial Revival church with many stained-glass windows, a hand-made altar, statues and other work by local craftsmen. Whether the church is open outside services was not checked.',
  'The church of Colorado\'s first Catholic parish, in the San Luis Valley.',
  'An active Catholic church at 6631-33 County Road 13, Conejos, just north of Antonito. Service times were not checked: no page of the parish\'s own could be read.',
  [W('Conejos, Colorado'),L('History Colorado: Our Lady of Guadalupe Church','https://historycolorado.org/node/56148')],brick='some'),
N('hope-lutheran-westcliffe','Hope Lutheran Church','Westcliffe','co','worship','standing',38.13352,-105.46753,'site','view','drive','','1917',
  'Designed and built in 1917 by the Reverend John Reininga, of hand-made concrete blocks. Its tower, 96 feet tall, is seen for miles across the Wet Mountain Valley. It was listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'A plain rectangular church whose only ornament is brick quoins at the corners (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'A 96-foot concrete-block tower over the Wet Mountain Valley.',
  'At 310 South 3rd Street, Westcliffe. Whether it still holds services or opens to visitors was not checked: look from the street.',
  [W('Hope Lutheran Church (Westcliffe, Colorado)')],brick='?'),
N('museum-of-colorado-prisons','Museum of Colorado Prisons (old women\'s prison)','Canon City','co','inst','standing',38.4394,-105.2473,'exact','open','drive','','1935',
  'The state women\'s prison of 1935 stands against the east wall of the Colorado Territorial Correctional Facility, a prison since 1871. Fremont County volunteers won the use of the cell house in the 1980s: the legislature agreed in 1986, Canon City gave a 99-year lease, and the museum opened on June 18, 1988.',
  'The cells were restored to keep the feel of the cell house, and hold artifacts of the Colorado prison system from 1871 on (Wikipedia). The old prison next door is still in use.',
  'A 1935 women\'s cell house beside a prison that has held inmates since 1871.',
  'A non-profit museum off U.S. 50 in Canon City (Wikipedia). Hours not checked: its own site could not be opened when this was written.',
  [W('Museum of Colorado Prisons')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('victor-downtown-co','Victor downtown historic district','Victor','co','town','standing',38.71083,-105.14028,'site','public','walk','','1891–',
  'Victor was founded in 1891 after Winfield Scott Stratton found gold nearby, and became a city in 1894; the Gold Coin Mine was struck while digging a building\'s foundation. With Cripple Creek it made the second-largest gold district in the country, and at its peak about 1900 it had some 18,000 people. Worn-out ore, labor strife and the loss of miners in the First World War emptied it; 379 lived there in 2020. The 22-acre downtown was listed on the National Register in 1985 with 55 contributing buildings.',
  'Several blocks between Diamond Avenue, Second, Portland and Fifth Streets, including the Victor Hotel and the Western Federation of Miners union hall (Wikipedia). The Victor Lowell Thomas Museum fills an 1899 building with its original wood floors and tin ceilings, two floors of mining-era exhibits (Pikes Peak Library District). Mining has resumed on Battle Mountain above the town. Museum hours not checked.',
  'A gold-rush city of 18,000 shrunk to a few hundred, its downtown still standing.',
  'Public streets in the City of Victor, Teller County. The museum is in the centre of town; its hours were not on the pages read.',
  [W('Victor, Colorado'),W('Victor Downtown Historic District'),L('Pikes Peak Library District: the Victor Lowell Thomas Museum','https://ppld.org/Victor-Museum')],brick='some'),
N('hotel-colorado-glenwood','Hotel Colorado','Glenwood Springs','co','hotel','standing',39.55086,-107.32469,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1891–1893',
  'The silver magnate and banker Walter Devereux began it in 1891 at a cost of $850,000, to designs by Boring & Tilton of New York modelled on the Villa Medici; it opened on June 10, 1893. Long visits by Theodore Roosevelt and William Howard Taft earned it the name "the little White House of the West".',
  'An Italianate hotel of cream-coloured Roman brick and Peach Blow sandstone (Wikipedia). A working hotel; its own history page tells of Taft\'s arrival in 1909 and keeps one tower suite as a tribute to a woman of its history. Interior beyond the lobby not checked.',
  'An 1893 copy of the Villa Medici where presidents stayed.',
  'A working hotel in Glenwood Springs (its own site, read November 2026).',
  [W('Hotel Colorado'),L('Hotel Colorado: history','https://www.hotelcolorado.com/history')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('stanley-hotel-estes-park','The Stanley Hotel','Estes Park','co','hotel','standing',40.38288,-105.51921,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1909',
  'Freelan Oscar Stanley, co-founder of the Stanley Motor Carriage Company, was stricken again with tuberculosis in 1903 and built this resort in Estes Park, which opened on July 4, 1909, for wealthy Easterners and as a health retreat; the main hotel and concert hall were finished in 1909 and the Manor in 1910. It was the inspiration for the Overlook Hotel in Stephen King\'s The Shining. In 2025 it was bought by a public-private partnership for $400 million.',
  'A Georgian Revival hotel of 140 rooms with an octagonal cupola and Palladian window; the main building, concert hall and Manor are steel-framed and clapboarded on rubble granite foundations, painted white about 1935 (first yellow ochre). The historic district has 11 contributing buildings (Wikipedia). The hotel runs 60-minute guided history, ghost and Shining tours.',
  'The 1909 resort that gave Stephen King the Overlook Hotel.',
  'A working hotel in Estes Park, about 5 miles from Rocky Mountain National Park. Tours are booked through the hotel (its tours page, read November 2026); tour times and prices not checked.',
  [W('The Stanley Hotel'),L('The Stanley Hotel: tours','https://www.stanleyhotel.com/tours')],brick='no',hours='Working hotel; guided 60-minute history and ghost tours (times not checked)'),
]
